window.WL_POSTS = [{"title":"5 Ways to Reduce Tension in Your Life","dek":"What is tension? What is the role of stress in our experience of tension?","cat":"stress","blocks":[{"t":"h3","x":"What is Tension?","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Tension, as it relates to our psychological experiences, can be defined as a state that is associated with conflict, dissonance, instability, or uncertainty. Psychological tension also creates a desire for resolution and feelings of expectation, anticipation, or prediction concerning future events that are potentially emotionally impactful (Lehne & Koelsch, 2015).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Tension is typically experienced in our bodies as tightness or stiffness in our muscles. This kind of tension can be quite painful and can sometimes severely restrict your ability to move. Tense muscles may be tender to the touch and feel like a chronic cramp or spasm.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"Where might we experience tension?","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Tension is a characteristic present in a variety of physical and emotional experiences. Here are a few examples of where we might observe tension:","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"li","x":"Tension and resolution in music, film, and literature","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Balance of opposing forces created in visual art","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Interpersonal conflict or hostility","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Experience of conflicting desires within ourselves","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Tension Headaches","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Neck and shoulder pain","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Anticipating an emotionally impactful event.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"h3","x":"Stress & Tension","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Our fears and anxieties don’t just occur in our minds, they are expressed throughout our bodies as well. When we are stressed, the branch of our nervous system called the sympathetic nervous system is activated. Activation of the sympathetic nervous system is the physiological component of our fight or flight response. That is, our sympathetic nervous system is responsible for preparing our bodies for action when we feel as though we are in danger.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Part of this preparation is the release of a neurotransmitter, called acetylcholine, which is responsible for making our muscles contract. Thus, when we are stressed out, our bodies interpret that stress as danger and activate our sympathetic nervous system, which promotes the release of acetylcholine, and ultimately leads to the contraction of muscles, even when we don’t want it to.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Research has shown that stress-induced muscle contractions, or muscle tension, are especially prominent in the face, neck, and shoulders (Bansevicius et al., 1997; Glaros et al., 2016). You might notice this effect if you clench your jaw or raise your shoulders when you are stressed. However, stress-induced muscle tension can occur throughout your body and isn’t necessarily localized to your head, neck, and shoulders.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"5  Ways to Reduce Tension in Your Life","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Because tension lives in our bodies, even when it’s born in our minds, the best way to reduce tension is through processes of physical relaxation. Anything that makes you feel relaxed could help, such as a walk with your dog, a conversation with a dear friend, or a cup of hot cocoa. In addition to the methods of relaxation that you know to be effective for you, there are multiple scientifically supported, non-pharmacological methods for achieving tension reduction through physical relaxation including Progressive Muscle Relaxation, yoga, heat therapy, cold therapy, and massage.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"li","x":"Reduce Tension With Progressive Muscle Relaxation","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"One of the most common and most scientifically supported methods of tension reduction is a practice called Progressive Muscle Relaxation (PMR). Progressive Muscle Relaxation involves the conscious tightening and releasing of muscles throughout your body. Studies have shown that even a brief Progressive Muscle Relaxation intervention can help reduce psychological stress and tension (Dolbier & Rush, 2012).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"The process of Progressive Muscle Relaxation typically goes like this:","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Start in a comfortable position, lying flat on your back if possible.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Focus first on your feet and contract every muscle in your feet with as much force as possible.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Hold for a few seconds, then release.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Focus next on your calves and contract your calf muscles with as much force as possible.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Hold for a few seconds, then release","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Repeat this process for each muscle group as your focus travels from your feet to your head.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"li","x":"Reduce Tension With Yoga","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"h3","x":"Gentle exercise and stretching can also be effective treatments for muscle tension. Yoga, for example, is a great way to combine both light exercise and stretching.","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Reduce Tension With Heat Therapy","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Heat promotes increased blood flow, metabolism, and elasticity of connective tissue. Increased blood flow and metabolism are thought to help with pain relief by accelerating the breakdown of pain-inducing toxins in the muscles and the distribution of nutrients our muscles need to heal (Tepperman & Devlin, 1986). Increased elasticity improves range of motion and decreases the feeling of stiffness. It’s important to be cautious about using heat therapy if you have multiple sclerosis, poor circulation, spinal cord injuries, diabetes, and arthritis because heat can contribute to disease progression and increased inflammation or could cause burns (Malanga et al., 2015). If you are unsure if heat therapy may be right for you, talking with your doctor is always a good idea.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"li","x":"Reduce Tension With Cold Therapy","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"While heat therapy promotes healing, cold therapy temporarily relieves pain. Cold leads to a decrease in inflammation and reduces the speed at which nerve fibers from your muscles can send pain signals to your brain (Tepperman & Devlin, 1986).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"li","x":"Reduce Tension With Massage Therapy","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Massage involves the application of pressure to the soft muscle tissue which causes an involuntary relaxation response. The relaxation response decreases activation of the sympathetic nervous system which results in a reduction in blood pressure and stress hormones. In other words, it decreases the physical effects of stress. Massage also promotes blood flow, circulation of lymphatic fluid, and the release of deep connective tissues, which reduces painful contractions and spasms (Yates, 2004).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"In Sum","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Tension is a common experience that can range from uncomfortable to debilitating. It is important to remember that our emotions live in our bodies, so even when we manage to ease the pain of muscle tension, we are still prone to experiencing more episodes of tension if we don’t manage our stressors. Because tension seems to exacerbate stress, easing the physical effects of tension is an important step in managing our stress, but it isn’t the only course of action. Truly finding lasting relief requires tending to both our bodies and our minds.  Please fill out the contact form or call 312.899.1120 to learn more about therapy for stress and anxiety.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"References","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Bansevicius, D., Westgaard, R. H., & Jensen, C. (1997). Mental stress of long duration: EMG activity, perceived tension, fatigue, and pain development in pain‐free subjects. Headache: The Journal of Head and Face Pain, 37(8), 499-510.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Dolbier, C. L., & Rush, T. E. (2012). Efficacy of abbreviated progressive muscle relaxation in a high-stress college sample. International Journal of Stress Management, 19(1), 48.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Glaros, A. G., Marszalek, J. M., & Williams, K. B. (2016). Longitudinal multilevel modeling of facial pain, muscle tension, and stress. Journal of dental research, 95(4), 416-422.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Lehne, M., & Koelsch, S. (2015). Toward a general psychological model of tension and suspense. Frontiers in psychology, 6, 79.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Malanga, G. A., Yan, N., & Stark, J. (2015). Mechanisms and efficacy of heat and cold therapies for musculoskeletal injury. Postgraduate medicine, 127(1), 57-65.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Tepperman, P. S., & Devlin, M. (1986). The therapeutic use of local heat and cold. Canadian Family Physician, 32, 1110.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Yates, John. (2004). A Physician's Guide to Therapeutic Massage, Third Edition. Ontario, Canada: Curties-Overzet Publications, Inc.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Wendi Lev, LCSW, ACSW, CADC","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true}],"read":"6 min read","num":"01"},{"title":"7 Ways to Stay Present","dek":"Strategies to help you get out of your head and start living more in the moment.","cat":"mindfulness","blocks":[{"t":"h3","x":"The Power Of Being Present","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"h3","x":"When people talk about staying present or living in the moment they are often talking about mindfulness, or “paying attention on purpose, in the present moment, and non-judgmentally to the unfolding of experience moment by moment” (Kabat‐Zinn, 2003). This type of mindful, present-focused living keeps you out of your head, stops you from ruminating on things that went wrong in the past, and lessens the amount of time you spend worrying about what could go wrong in the future. As a result, living in the moment can enable you to enjoy the moment more and feel like your life is more meaningful or fulfilling.","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"In fact, these types of interventions have been shown to have positive effects on both anxiety and depression (Khoury et al, 2013).  That's in part because when we get stuck in our heads we might not be able to notice the good things that are right in front of us.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"But learning how to be more present isn't always easy. If we are the kind of person whose mind wanders and we are always imagining or thinking about something, how do we pull ourselves back to the present moment? Here are some strategies and techniques to try.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"1. Try Mindfulness Meditation","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Guided mindfulness meditations can help you build the skills that make it easier to live in the present moment. They do this by teaching you to focus on your bodily sensation and your breathing. By practicing focusing on your breath, you are really practicing focusing on the present moment. Below is a mindfulness meditation for you to try.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"https://youtu.be/cVeyjwnxlIA","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"2. Try the Raisin Exercise","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"The raisin exercise involves using all five senses to explore a raisin. First, observe its appearance, noticing each crevice. Then notice how it feels in your hand. Then put it up to your nose and notice its smell. Then put the raisin on your tongue and pay attention to how it feels. Then bite into it and notice how it tastes. Finally, chew and swallow it, noticing how it feels going into your body. This exercise can help you become more aware of your senses and physical experiences. You can extend this skill by observing other things in your life to help yourself be more present.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"3. Practice Gratitude","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Gratitude is thought to be an orientation towards noticing and appreciating the positive things in the world. Gratitude is strongly related to well-being (Wood, Froh, & Geraghty, 2010), and it can't exist without first living in the present moment. That's because if we're not present, we won't notice all the things around us that we might be grateful for—things like the smell of fresh-cut grass, the feel of the wind on our cheeks, or the sight of a flower growing between sidewalk squares in the middle of a city. By working to practice gratitude, we'll help cultivate our \"awareness\" skills and as a result, be more present and appreciative.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"4. Stop Rumination Cycles","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Rumination is \"a mode of responding to distress that involves repetitively and passively focusing on symptoms of distress and on the possible causes and consequences of these symptoms\" (Nolen-Hoeksema, Wisco, & Lyubomirsky, 2008). When we ruminate, we stay fixated on our problems, our feelings, or our experiences, and we don't take action in the present to resolve them. As a result, rumination is associated with greater levels of depression and anxiety.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"When we live in the present, we may still have negative emotions about things that happen to us. But instead of turning them over and over again in our minds, we take action to change those emotions in the moment. For example, if we're mad about something our romantic partner did, instead of thinking about how rude they were, we go and talk to them, tell them how we feel, and request that they behave differently next time.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"5. Try Box Breathing","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Box breathing is a type of controlled breathing that involves taking slow breaths in and out.  Focusing on our breathing helps us practice focusing on something that is happening in the now while also calming down our physiology and reducing stress.  If you want to try box breathing, check out the video below to be guided through it.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"https://youtu.be/tEmt1Znux58","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"6. Try The Body Scan Meditation","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"The body scan exercise involves bringing attention to your body. Start by closing your eyes and shifting from one region of the body to the next, paying attention to any sensations you notice. For example, do you notice tightness in your back, a knot in your stomach, or an itch on your foot? By practicing paying attention to what's going on inside of you at this moment, you can build your skills and hopefully make it easier for yourself to stay present.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"7. Take Mindful Photos","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Maybe you're someone who has a hard time keeping your mind from buzzing. Then maybe it'll help you to use a tool to stay present. Try snapping a few photos each day. Focus on taking pictures of things you never noticed before. Hmm, I never realized that house was red. I can't believe I never noticed that my dog has a spot that looks like Elvis. Or, wow, that candy has a quote on its wrapper that I never saw before. By taking these photos we may be able to help remind ourselves to notice things more.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"Final Thoughts on Living in the Moment","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Living in the present moment is a great skill that can be extremely helpful for boosting mental health and well-being. Luckily, there are a number of ways to build and grow this skill. Hopefully, these tips here provided you with some activities that are a good fit and make a positive impact on your life.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"References","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Kabat‐Zinn, J. (2003). Mindfulness‐based interventions in context: past, present, and future. Clinical psychology: Science and practice, 10(2), 144-156.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Khoury, B., Lecomte, T., Fortin, G., Masse, M., Therien, P., Bouchard, V., ... & Hofmann, S. G. (2013). Mindfulness-based therapy: a comprehensive meta-analysis. Clinical psychology review, 33(6), 763-771.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Nolen-Hoeksema, S., Wisco, B. E., & Lyubomirsky, S. (2008). Rethinking rumination. Perspectives on psychological science, 3(5), 400-424.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Wood, A. M., Froh, J. J., & Geraghty, A. W. (2010). Gratitude and well-being: A review and theoretical integration. PClinical psychology review, 30(7), 890-","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Wendi Lev, LCSW  8:10/35","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true}],"read":"5 min read","num":"02"},{"title":"7 Relationship Red Flags To Watch Out For","dek":"Discover what red flags in a relationship are, why it is important to recognize them, and how to proceed when you do find some.","cat":"relationships","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Red flags – in interpersonal relationships, to be specific – are behaviors or characteristics from another person that suggest you may not want to spend time with them (Csajbok et al., 2023). This may be because they will be unpleasant, uninteresting, or even dangerous; while the negative outcomes that could result from interacting with that person will vary, what is clear is that these red flags are omens of worse behaviors to come (Kearney & O’Brien, 2021).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"The defining essence of a red flag is that it indicates future harm or unpleasantness should you keep spending time with the person (Csajbok et al., 2023). In this way, red flags can also be understood as characteristics or behaviors that helpfully signal to us, “stay away” – this way, we can avoid investing further in relationships that are not good for us. Red flags stop that investment before it gets harder to disengage from the relationship (Jonason et al., 2015).","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"All interactions between people feature some degree of both reward and risk. Red flags are important because they clearly signal potential risks in the situation (Csajbok et al., 2023). We are constantly evaluating our environments for signs of reward and risk (Britton et al., 2011); potentially rewarding things motivate us to come closer, while potentially risky things give us pause. Red flags cause us to hesitate – they activate our threat appraisal systems.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"Examples of Red Flags","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Researchers have tried to categorize red flags, or what they sometimes call “dealbreakers”. One such study produced a number of categories from which we can draw helpful examples, at least for the domain of romantic relationships (Jonason et al., 2015):","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"li","x":"Unattractiveness. It is hard to sustain interest in a person we find physically unattractive.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Unhealthy Lifestyle. It might be extremely challenging to be in a relationship with a person who is not very interested in experiencing the world in the ways that you like.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Undesirable Personality Traits. For example, someone who repeatedly interrupts you is probably either disinterested or unable to pay attention. Either way, if you continue to interact with the other person, you may not feel heard.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Differing Religious Beliefs. Some people say that they could never date somebody who believes in God, and others insist that they can only date somebody who is religious. This might be related to fears that somebody with different religious beliefs will not share important core values with us (Furnham, 2009).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Limited Social Status. While it may not sound fair or very charitable, people who have few social connections are often seen as undesirable.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Divergent Mating Psychologies. You have probably heard a friend relate how much they enjoyed a date… until they realized the other person was interested in more (or less!) commitment than they were.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Differing Relationship Goals. On a similar note, people may match up in many different ways, but if they do not share a similar philosophy toward having children, or how to balance professional versus personal priorities, they may recognize this incompatibility as a key red flag.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"h2","x":"Red Flags in Relationships","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Relationship red flags can signal more than “this isn’t the person for me.” Red flags also highlight the possibility that somebody is actually a threat to us (Kearney & O’Brien, 2021). Two researchers even developed a measure of relationship red flags, with the goal of relating those behaviors to people’s risk of experiencing dating violence (Kearney & O’Brien, 2021). They found five categories of red flag behavior and showed that people who recognized these behaviors as red flags were less accepting of dating violence:","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"li","x":"Monitoring: When a family member or romantic partner wants to know exactly where you are and what you are doing at all times, they are monitoring you far beyond what is typical in relationships.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Controlling: Another red flag is attempting to control behaviors of yours that should be yours to decide. Whether or not you see friends and family members, when you go to the grocery store, and how long you talk to people on the phone are decisions you get to make, not your partner.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Demeaning: Any comments that are truly insulting or dismissive suggest a lack of respect and care for you, which will probably show up in other ways over time, too.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Threatening and Aggressive: Where there is aggression of one kind (for example, warning you there will be consequences if you do something), there is likely to be aggression of another kind eventually.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Jealous and Possessive: Finally, it is a clear red flag when one person does not want the other to live a fulfilling and independent life of their own.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"h2","x":"In Sum","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"There are generally two categories of red flags you might encounter: those that clearly demonstrate a person is unsafe or unhealthy to be around, and those that demonstrate that they are not a good fit for you, the perceiver of the red flags. Hopefully this article has made it clear what general red flags are and given you an occasion to think about what red flags of incompatibility are for you specifically. There is nothing wrong with knowing what you do and do not want from relationships! Let your intuition guide you when you sense something is off – you can identify the red flags.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Wendi Lev, LCSW","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true}],"read":"4 min read","num":"03"},{"title":"How to Answer \"What Do I Want to Do With My Life?\"","dek":"Not sure what you want? Here are some important questions you can ask yourself.","cat":"purpose","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"\"What do I want to do with my life?\" is a question we all ask ourselves at some point. We wonder: What career do we want? How do we want to spend our time? What really leads to a life worth living?","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"The answer to what we want to do with our lives depends on a number of things. So let's talk about some of the questions you might ask yourself to find your answer.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"1. What makes you happy?","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"We all want happiness. We want to experience positive emotions and eudaimonia—or meaning in life. So when it comes to figuring out what we really want in life, we might first ask ourselves what makes us happy.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"For example, what do we like to do? When are we the happiest? Who are we with when we are the happiest? What goals bring a smile to our faces? Now, what kind of life would help you do these things and feel this way more often?","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"2. What are your needs?","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Next, it can be helpful to ask ourselves which needs are most important to us right now. Now, be careful not to confuse needs with wants. We might want a million dollars, while we might need financial security. We might want the perfect partner, but we might need a partner who loves us and treats us well.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"According to Maslow's hierarchy of needs, social/love needs and self-esteem needs are of higher priority than self-actualization—or living up to our full potential. Other needs—like competence, autonomy, and relatedness—are also thought to be keys to well-being and living a good life (Reis et al., 2000).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"3. What are your values?","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Values serve as guiding principles that help us move forward in ways that matter to us (Roccas, Sagiv, Schwartz, & Knafo, 2002). So reflecting on our values can help move us in the right direction. We might value social connection, and that helps us see that what we want in life involves being around others. Or, we might value kindness, and that shows that what we want in life may be a career helping others. By reflecting on your core values, you can better understand what you want.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"4. What activities do you get absorbed in?","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"You know that feeling when you are so absorbed in your work or activities that you lose track of time? That feeling is referred to as flow—or the positive feeling of being totally connected to our performance (Jackson & Marsh, 1996). Flow occurs when we're doing things we really love that are just the right fit for our skill level.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"So, what are the activities you get super absorbed in? Knowing the answer to this question can give you clues about what you want in life.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"5. What would you do if you could do anything in life?","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"I don't like to get people's hopes and expectations up too much—the truth is we won't be able to reach every wildest dream we might think up. But on the flip side, we often place limits on our own potential that don't need to be there. So, taking the time to at least acknowledge what you really want can help you think about ways to move in that general direction.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"6. What is the gestalt of your life?","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Gestalt is German for \"pattern,\" \"shape,\" or \"configuration.\" In psychology, gestalt refers to the idea of a sort of picture—the different parts produce a whole. In life, we often focus a lot on the little things we might want to change—the job, the house, the car—without focusing as much on the overall picture of our lives. So when thinking about what we want in life, we may benefit from taking a step back.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"So ask yourself, what kind of life do you want to lead? What kind of feelings does this life have? How will the pieces fit together? What does it look like when you look at it from the outside? Asking these questions can hopefully help you understand more about what you want in life.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"References","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Reis, H. T., Sheldon, K. M., Gable, S. L., Roscoe, J., & Ryan, R. M. (2000). Daily well-being: The role of autonomy, competence, and relatedness. Personality and social psychology bulletin, 26(4), 419-435.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Roccas, S., Sagiv, L., Schwartz, S. H., & Knafo, A. (2002). The big five personality factors and personal values. Personality and social psychology bulletin, 28(6), 789-801.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Jackson, S. A., & Marsh, H. W. (1996). Development and validation of a scale to measure optimal experience: The Flow State Scale. Journal of sport and exercise psychology, 18(1), 17-35.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false}],"read":"4 min read","num":"04"},{"title":"How to Learn Better by Understanding Neuroplasticity","dek":"Keep reading to learn about this essential brain function that gives life its flavor.","cat":"brain","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Imagine a world in which we are unable to learn from our mistakes, we never take up a new hobby or learn a new skill, and we can’t learn to navigate a new city. This is a world without neuroplasticity. Neuroplasticity is an amazing brain function and one that we are critically dependent on in our daily lives. Let’s talk more about what neuroplasticity is and how it works.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"What Is Neuroplasticity?","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"h2","x":"Neuroplasticity is our brain’s ability to adapt in response to experience. In other words, it’s our ability to learn new things or develop new skills. As we move through life, we have to develop behaviors that suit our context and help us survive and sometimes thrive. The brain accomplishes this by creating new connections between neurons, thus altering the way different parts of the brain talk to each other, which ultimately supports the new behavior or skill (Kleim & Jones, 2008).","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Neuroplasticity might be one of the brain’s most important functions. If the neural circuitry that is created throughout our early development was fixed, we wouldn’t be able to adapt to new contexts or environments, the functional effects of brain damage would be permanent, and we wouldn’t be able to experience the joy of learning. We would be stuck as the person we were when our brains stopped developing. So essentially all humans would cognitively be 20-somethings forever.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"How Does Neuroplasticity Work?","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"There’s still much to be learned about neuroplasticity, but there are a few things that have been consistently observed in research experiments. Current research shows that neuroplasticity involves several types of changes that occur in the brain.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"One kind of change is in the actual structure of brain cells. For example, a neuroimaging study found structural changes in brain areas associated with learning and memory after a task that engaged learning and memory processes (Sagi et al., 2012).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Another kind of structural change observed in brain cells is something called dendritic growth. Dendrites are branch-like projections that extend from one cell and connect to another one to allow for communication between them. This communication comes in the form of a synapse, which is essentially just the flow of information from one neuron to another.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Neurons can have multiple dendrites that branch out in all directions and synapse on many other neighboring cells. More dendrites mean a single neuron can talk to more neurons at one time. Dendritic growth is the growth and branching of a neuron’s dendrites which leads to changes in the way different parts of the brain are able to communicate with each other. These changes in communication can lead to changes in neuronal networks—or an assembly of neurons that work together to support some function. And it's these changes that we observe as neuroplasticity (Ploughman et al., 2015).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"Principles of Neuroplasticity","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Researchers have identified 10 different principles of neuroplasticity (Kleim & Jones, 2008). These include:","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"li","x":"Use It or Lose It","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Use It and Improve It","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Specificity","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Repetition Matters","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Intensity Matters","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Time Matters","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Salience Matters","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Age Matters","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Transference or Generalization","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Interference","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Let’s explore what a few of these principles are.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"Use It or Lose It","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"This is a phrase that you are likely already familiar with, but it is a good encapsulation of one aspect of neuroplasticity. As we saw in the examples of neuroplasticity in blind people and deaf people, the brain isn’t a fan of letting areas go unused. The use it or lose it principle refers to this same process. However, unlike in these examples, the neural networks that are not being used aren’t necessarily repurposed for something better. Luckily, even if we don’t use it and we subsequently lose it, we can get it back through dedicated training and consistency.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"Intensity Matters","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"The intensity principle can be thought of as the dosage required to achieve a result. This will likely depend heavily on what you are trying to do and may not necessarily apply to your goals. For example, let’s say you are trying to quit a bad habit. There’s no way for you to intensely not do something. But if you are learning to play piano, sitting down for five minutes and playing one of those songs you can play with two fingers will not improve your piano-playing ability. Sitting down for an hour and challenging yourself with complicated pieces, on the other hand, will improve your ability to play the piano.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"Age Matters","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"This principle refers to the fact that our brains are much more plastic (or adaptable) when we are young than when we are old. Though an aging brain is still more than capable of learning new things, it just might not come as quickly or as easily as it would to a younger person.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"In Sum","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Neuroplasticity is one brain function we can all appreciate. It is the basis for so much of what makes life rich and enjoyable. Researchers are still discovering the mechanisms that underlie how it works, but it is clear that neuroplasticity plays a huge role in our lives.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"References","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"li","x":"Kleim, J. A., & Jones, T. A. (2008). Principles of experience-dependent neural plasticity: implications for rehabilitation after brain damage.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Ploughman, M., Austin, M. W., Glynn, L., & Corbett, D. (2015). The effects of poststroke aerobic exercise on neuroplasticity: a systematic review of animal and clinical studies. Translational stroke research, 6, 13–28.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Sagi, Y., Tavor, I., Hofstetter, S., Tzur-Moryosef, S., Blumenfeld-Katzir, T., & Assaf, Y. (2012). Learning in the fast lane: new insights into neuroplasticity. Neuron, 73(6), 1195-1203.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false}],"read":"4 min read","num":"05"},{"title":"18 Quotes About Gratitude for Everything Life Throws at You","dek":"Discover gratitude quotes that will inspire you to live thankfully.","cat":"gratitude","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Noticing the comfort of a warm breeze on a cool day, pausing to appreciate the fragrance of jasmine blooming on the vine, telling someone you love them, and thanking a friend for their thoughtful advice are all examples of gratitude. Gratitude is essentially noticing and appreciating the good in our lives.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"It’s been defined as “the willingness to recognize the unearned increments of value in one's experience” (Bertocci & Millard, 1963, p. 389). Research has shown that getting good at gratitude can precipitate staggering improvements in many aspects of our lives. For example, Emmons & McCullough (2003) showed that people who listed their blessings every week for 10 weeks experienced greater feelings of connection with others and were more satisfied with their life as a whole than people who only listed events or hassles. Other scientifically supported effects of gratitude include:","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"li","x":"Protection against depression and post-traumatic stress disorder (Van Dusen et al., 2015)","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Better quality of sleep (Wood et al., 2009)","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Enhanced self-perception (Geraghty et al., 2010)","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Being nicer (DeWall et al., 2012)","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"h3","x":"But keeping a consistent gratitude practice can be challenging. It’s all too easy to focus on our worries, fears, and disappointments. In times when we are struggling to recognize the value of gratitude or to find something to appreciate, the thoughts and reflections on gratitude of other people can help provide a richer perspective and reignite our motivation to live gratefully. Here is a collection of quotes on gratitude to refer to whenever you need them.","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“Be grateful every day for the brief but magnificent opportunity that life provides.” – Carl Sagan","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"\"Life is strange, beautiful, and terrifying…and I am thankful for every minute of it that I have had. I am thankful for every minute yet to come.\" – Benjamin W. Bass","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"\"Gratitude helps you to grow and expand; gratitude brings joy and laughter into your life and into the lives of all those around you.\" – Eileen Caddy","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"\"In the modern world, we are surrounded by so much abundance that we cannot see it.\" – Chris Matakas","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“Reflect on your present blessings, on which every man has many, not on your past misfortunes, of which all men have some.” – Charles Dickens","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“Gratitude can transform common days into thanksgivings, turn routine jobs into joy, and change ordinary opportunities into blessings.” – William Arthur Ward","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"\"Gratitude can transform any situation. It alters your vibration, moving you from negative energy to positive. It's the quickest, easiest, most powerful way to effect change in your life—this I know for sure.\" – Oprah Winfrey","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“Gratitude unlocks all that’s blocking us from really feeling truthful, really feeling authentic and vulnerable and happy.” – Gabrielle Bernstein","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“May the work of your hands be a sign of gratitude and reverence to the human condition.” – Mahatma Gandhi","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“Gratitude unlocks the fullness of life. It turns what we have into enough, and more. It turns denial into acceptance, chaos to order, confusion to clarity. It can turn a meal into a feast, a house into a home, a stranger into a friend.” – Melody Beattie","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“Life has no blessing like a prudent friend.” – Euripides","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“Let us be grateful to the people who make us happy; they are the charming gardeners who make our souls blossom.” —Marcel Proust","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“I have learned that to be with those I like is enough.” ― Walt Whitman","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“In ordinary life, we hardly realize that we receive a great deal more than we give, and that it is only with gratitude that life becomes rich.” – Dietrich Bonhoeffer","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“If you fail to carry around with you a heart of gratitude for the love you’ve been so freely given, it is easy for you not to love others as you should.” – Paul David Tripp","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“Demonstrate gratitude for the simple things in life. Show your child that you are a person who is truly grateful for the simple, good things in life: a sunset, rain, insects, birds, children laughing, or a kind gesture… Appreciative people are happy; they see the beauty in life while others miss it.” – Richard Carlson","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"\"When you arise in the morning, think of what a precious privilege it is to be alive—to breathe, to think, to enjoy, to love—then make that day count!\" – Steve Maraboli","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"\"Enjoy the little things, for one day you may look back and realize they were the big things.\" – Robert Brault","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"h3","x":"In Sum","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Practicing gratitude can help us live more meaningful, fulfilling, and enjoyable lives. The opportunities to find something to be grateful for are limitless when we are open and observant. It’s important to note that practicing gratitude doesn’t imply ignoring the painful parts of your life. Rather, gratitude entails recognizing that life can be both awful and beautiful at the same time. Holding both truths is challenging, but it is possible. And with practice, it will begin to come naturally.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"References","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Bertocci, P. A., & Millard, R. M. (1963). Personality and the good: Psychological and ethical perspectives. New York: McKay.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"DeWall, C. N., Lambert, N. M., Pond, R. S., Kashdan, T. B., & Fincham, F. D. (2012). A Grateful Heart is a Nonviolent Heart: Cross-Sectional, Experience Sampling, Longitudinal, and Experimental Evidence. Social Psychological and Personality Science, 3(2), 232–240.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Emmons, R. A., & McCullough, M. E. (2003). Counting blessings versus burdens: an experimental investigation of gratitude and subjective well-being in daily life. Journal of personality and social psychology, 84(2), 377.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Geraghty, A.W.A., Wood, A.M., Hyland, M.E., (2010). Attrition from self-directed interventions: Investigating the relationship between psychological predictors, intervention content and dropout from a body dissatisfaction intervention. Soc. Sci. Med. 71 (1), 30–37.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Van Dusen, J. P., Tiamiyu, M. F., Kashdan, T. B., & Elhai, J. D. (2015). Gratitude, depression and PTSD: Assessment of structural relationships. Psychiatry research, 230(3), 867-870.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Wood, A. M., Joseph, S., Lloyd, J., & Atkins, S. (2009). Gratitude influences sleep through the mechanism of pre-sleep cognitions. Journal of psychosomatic research, 66(1), 43-48.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false}],"read":"5 min read","num":"06"},{"title":"40 Quotes to Help You Be More Kind","dek":"Whether you need kindness yourself or want some guidance on how to be more kind, here are some wise words to get you started.","cat":"kindness","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"When life gets in the way and we find ourselves overwhelmed with its challenges, it can be difficult to remember the importance of being kind to our fellow human beings and ourselves. Worry not, for in this article, we will provide you with a list of quotes about kindness that can help inspire you to be just a little bit kinder to the people around you. So without further ado, let’s get inspired.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"li","x":"“If you see someone without a smile, give them yours” - Dolly Parton","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“If you want to lift yourself, lift someone else.” - Booker T. Washington","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“Be kind whenever possible. It is always possible.” - The 14th Dalai Lama.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“A kind gesture can reach a wound that only compassion can heal.” - Steve Maraboli","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“Human kindness has never weakened the stamina or softened the fiber of a free people.”  - Franklin D. Roosevelt.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“Always be a little kinder than necessary.” - James M. Barrie","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“To err on the side of kindness is seldom an error.” - Liz Armbruster","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“When words are both true and kind, they can change the world.” - Buddha","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“Kindness can become its motive. We are made kind by being kind.” - Eric Hoffer","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“Kindness is the golden chain by which society is bound together.” - Johann Wolfgang von Goethe.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“Sometimes it takes only one act of kindness and caring to change a person’s life.” - Jackie Chan","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“Be kind, for everyone you meet is fighting a hard battle.” - Philo of Alexandria","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“Kind words don’t cost much. Yet they accomplish much.” - Blaise Pasca","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“We are all neighbors. Be kind. Be gentle.” - Clemantine Wamariya","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“Never lose a chance of saying a kind word.” - William Makepeace Thackeray","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“Try to be a rainbow in someone else’s cloud.” - Maya Angelou","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“No act of kindness, no matter how small, is ever wasted.” - Aesop","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“We should model the kindness we want to see.” - Brené Brown","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“Be nice to strangers. Be nice even when it doesn’t matter.” - Sam Altman","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“There is nothing more truly artistic than to love people.” - Vincent Van Gogh","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“Compassion isn’t about solutions. It’s about giving all the love that you’ve got.” - Cheryl Strayed.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“A part of kindness consists in loving people more than they deserve.” - Joseph Joubert","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“What we all have in common is an appreciation of kindness and compassion; all the religions have this. Love. We all lean towards love.” - Richard Gere","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“Whatever possession we gain by our sword cannot be sure or lasting, but the love gained by kindness and moderation is certain and durable.” - Alexander the Great","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“The best portion of a good man's life is his little, nameless, unremembered acts of kindness and of love.” - William Wordsworth","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“Love and compassion are necessities, not luxuries. Without them, humanity cannot survive.” - Dalai Lama","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“A tree is known by its fruit; a man by his deeds. A good deed is never lost; he who sows courtesy reaps friendship, and he who plants kindness gathers love.” - Saint Basil","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“Every act of kindness is a piece of love we leave behind.” - Paul Williams","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“Love and kindness are never wasted. They always make a difference. They bless the one who receives them, and they bless you, the giver.” - Barbara De Angelis","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“Kindness in words creates confidence. Kindness in thinking creates profoundness. Kindness in giving creates love.” - Lao Tzu","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"We can’t heal the world today, but we can begin with a voice of compassion, a heart of love, an act of kindness.” - Mary Davis","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“Kindness is loving yourself enough to love those around you.” - Raktivist","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Self-compassion is simply giving the same kindness to ourselves that we would give to others. - Christopher Germer","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“Remember, you have been criticizing yourself for years and it hasn’t worked. Try approving of yourself and see what happens.” - Louise L. Hay","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“Drop the people who do not value you, respect you. Life has infinite horizons. Accept yourself, love yourself, and move forward.” - Amit Ray","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“Be kind to your body, gentle with your mind and patient with your heart. Stay true to your spirit, cherish your soul and never doubt yourself. You are still becoming, my love, and there is no one more deserving of the nurturing grace of your love.” - Becca Lee","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“When you say, ‘Yes’ to others make sure you are not saying ‘No’ to yourself.” - Paulo Coelho","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“You yourself, as much as anybody in the entire universe, deserve your love and affection.” - Sharon Salzberg","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“Let go of the harsh self-judgment, and start replacing those with loving thoughts about yourself. Learn to be kind to yourself, and your outside world will change as well.” - Anita Moorjani","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“Learn to be kind to yourself. It’s hard to be happy when there’s always someone being mean to you.” - Christine Arylo","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"h3","x":"In Sum","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"How did it feel to read over these quotes? Did any of these words catch your eye? Are there any quotes you wrote down to remember and recite later? In a world where the light can sometimes feel like it is dimming, we hope these words of kindness offered a bright light to your day. As many of these quotes suggested, kindness is a domino effect. We hope you take the chance to show kindness to yourself and the people around you in hopes that a small act travels far and wide.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Wendi Lev, LCSW, ACSW, CADC 7/09/2025","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true}],"read":"4 min read","num":"07"},{"title":"Discover the Consequences of Stereotyping","dek":"Learn about the assumptions we make about people based on their belonging to certain social groups.","cat":"social","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Stereotypes are quick judgments we make about other people based on a single characteristic they possess (Bodenhausen et al., 1994). They reflect the associations we have between that particular characteristic and other personality traits we think the person might possess. Stereotypes are simplistic judgments we make instead of considering the other person as a unique individual about whom we can make no assumptions.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Stereotypes are a form of social categorization. We have a natural and automatic tendency to try to classify people into groups (Fiske, 1998). This tendency would disappear if it was not at least somewhat effective, and indeed, the assumptions we make about other people based on single traits are not always harmful. However, stereotyping is not fully automatic; we can and should recognize when we are engaged in making such assumptions as well as the situations that make us more likely to stereotype (Fiske, 1998).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"This is because stereotyping entails believing that members of a certain group always behave a certain way, or have a certain trait, as a result of belonging to that group. For example, two stereotypes sometimes held about Jewish people are that they are greedy and that they are politically liberal (Berinsky & Mendelberg, 2005). The first stereotype has become far less acceptable to voice, while the second might be considered a more reasonable assumption by some people. However, neither stereotype is a fair assumption to make about any particular Jewish person.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"Causes of Stereotyping","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"It is a fundamental human need to understand where we stand in relation to other people (Brewer, 1999). This could mean needing to know that we do or do not belong to a group or whether another person comes in peace or is a threat. Regardless of the social motivation, we make these judgments in less than a second (Ito & Urland, 2003) and act accordingly. This ability to read other people can make us incredibly smooth and effective social operators—think of a natural politician such as Barack Obama working a crowd—but it also sets us up to think and behave in prejudicial ways.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"We can use developmental intergroup theory (Bigler & Liben, 2006) to explain how we come to stereotype other people. This theory proposes that because we are such good categorizers, and because it is so essential for functioning effectively in the world, we learn from an early age to look for differences between people. Over time, we notice patterns in those differences and begin to classify people into groups.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"Consequences of Stereotyping","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Stereotypes have a full range of consequences. Though some can be relatively benign, many are extremely harmful. Even just anticipating being stereotyped by others can affect people’s athletic and academic performance, financial decision making, and performance at work (Spencer et al., 2016b). For a good example of how holding stereotypes can lead to negative consequences, we need look no further than the example of racial stereotypes affecting decision making among police officers, which has led to many needless deaths and high levels of political and social upheaval (Spencer et al., 2016a).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Stereotypes also impact our workplaces, leading to unequal treatment of employees when it comes to job reviews, promotions, and hiring (Operario & Fiske, 2001). Many efforts have been made to address stereotypes within professional settings because workplaces are systems where leadership can exert a stronger influence over the company’s culture, and some interventions do seem to help reduce the impacts of stereotypes on workplace outcomes.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"Stereotyping Theory","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"A lot of what psychologists think about stereotyping boils down to this: It is sometimes an effective and highly efficient way of thinking, so we keep doing it (Wegener et al., 2006). Our brains are always trying to be as efficient as possible, and they seem willing to sacrifice some accuracy for the sake of efficiency. In other words, we stereotype because we can often get aw were ay with making instant judgments of people and situations.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Social psychology research tells us that stereotyping is a behavior that is rooted in our social contexts as much as it is in our brains (Amodio, 2014). When we must make decisions under time pressure, or when we are presented with particularly threatening situations, we are more likely to stereotype other people. Our thinking becomes even more automatic, and less reflectful, when the older, more survival-focused parts of our brains get activated (Amodio, 2014).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"In a moment like this, the theory suggests, we use our preconceptions about a given group to quickly evaluate the person we are interacting with and take action. It seems like there just isn’t the time to more thoughtfully evaluate the person or the situation, and we seem to have good reason to believe the preconceptions are real or effective. Indeed, it is rather effortful to override this automatic and mostly unconscious cognitive pattern (Amodio, 2014). And in fact, many people believe stereotypes not just unconsciously but consciously as well (Wegener et al., 2006).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"In Sum","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Stereotyping is a very natural human tendency—one that unfortunately comes with plenty of negative consequences. When we are the targets of stereotyping, hopefully we can recognize that it is not personal and often done unconsciously. At the same time, we all can also take responsibility for the moments when we slip into stereotypical thought.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"References","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Amodio, D. M. (2014). The neuroscience of prejudice and stereotyping. Nature Reviews Neuroscience, 15(10), 670–682.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Berinsky, A. J., & Mendelberg, T. (2005). The indirect effects of discredited stereotypes in judgments of Jewish leaders. American Journal of Political Science, 49(4), 845–864.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Bigler, R. S., & Liben, L. S. (2006). A developmental intergroup theory of social stereotypes and prejudice. Advances in Child Development and Behavior, 34, 39–89.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Bodenhausen, G. V., Sheppard, L. A., & Kramer, G. P. (1994). Negative affect and social judgment: The differential impact of anger and sadness. European Journal of Social Psychology, 24(1), 45–62.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Brewer, M. B. (1999). The psychology of prejudice: Ingroup love and outgroup hate? Journal of Social Issues, 55(3), 429–444.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Eagly, A. H., & Wood, W. (2012). Social role theory. In P. A. M. Van Lange, A. W. Kruglanski, & E. T. Higgins (Eds.), Handbook of theories of social psychology (pp. 458–476). Sage Publications Ltd.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Fiske, S. T. (1998). Stereotyping, prejudice, and discrimination. In D. T. Gilbert, S. T. Fiske, & G. Lindzey (Eds.), The handbook of social psychology (pp. 357–411). McGraw-Hill.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Fiske, S. T., Cuddy, A. J., Glick, P., & Xu, J. (2002). A model of (often mixed) stereotype content: competence and warmth respectively follow from perceived status and competition. Journal of Personality and Social Psychology, 82(6), 878–902.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Ito, T. A., & Urland, G. R. (2003). Race and gender on the brain: electrocortical measures of attention to the race and gender of multiply categorizable individuals. Journal of Personality and Social Psychology, 85(4), 616–626.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Operario, D., & Fiske, S. T. (2001). Causes and consequences of stereotypes in organizations. In M. London (Ed.), How people evaluate others in organizations (pp. 45–62). Lawrence Erlbaum Associates Publishers.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Spencer, K. B., Charbonneau, A. K., & Glaser, J. (2016a). Implicit bias and policing. Social and Personality Psychology Compass, 10(1), 50–63.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Spencer, S. J., Logel, C., & Davies, P. G. (2016b). Stereotype threat. Annual Review of Psychology, 67, 415–437.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Wegener, D. T., Clark, J. K., & Petty, R. E. (2006). Not all stereotyping is created equal: Differential consequences of thoughtful versus nonthoughtful stereotyping. Journal of Personality and Social Psychology, 90(1), 42–59.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false}],"read":"6 min read","num":"08"},{"title":"Better Understand Your Child’s Cognitive Development Through the Work of Jean Piaget","dek":"Let’s explore Jean Piaget’s highly influential work on cognitive development—the study of how children’s thinking changes as they grow.","cat":"development","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Jean Piaget (1896–1980) was a Swiss psychologist known for his pioneering work in the field of developmental psychology. He is best known for his theory of cognitive development, which describes how children learn and acquire knowledge.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"He is famous for disputing the existing theories of cognitive development of his time, which asserted that children’s knowledge was innate. Instead, he thought that a child's comprehension and awareness of the world evolved as a result of the child's interactions with it.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"Why Is Piaget Important?","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Piaget is important because he developed the first major theory of cognitive development, describing how children’s ability to think and reason grows and changes (Miller, 2011). Although Piaget's theory has been criticized on some points, it remains one of the most important and influential theories of cognitive development. Two areas where his theory influenced huge changes are child psychology and education.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Child Psychology","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Before Piaget, the study of cognition was mainly on adults. During that time, psychologists saw children as “mini adults” and believed they thought in the same way adults do. However, Piaget's work showed that children think very differently from adults and that their thinking changes in a predictable way as they mature.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"His theory has changed the way we evaluate children in different age groups. For example, with a 6-year-old, a clinician won’t be alarmed that the child can't grasp abstract concepts. However, if an adolescent can't understand others’ viewpoints or hypothetical situations, it’s maybe time for concern (Scott & Cogburn, 2023). This understanding also helps to determine if a child has issues with cognitive development such as attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder, autism spectrum disorders, or a learning disability (Malik & Marwaha, 2023).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Education","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Piaget's stages of cognitive development influenced our understanding of children's learning by emphasizing the importance of age-appropriate, child-centered education. It emphasizes the importance of tailoring educational practices to the cognitive abilities of children at different developmental stages. His work has been cited in over 20,000 studies, and it has been used to develop new curricula and teaching methods.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Piaget's theory is based on the idea that children actively construct their knowledge through interactions with their environment. Because of this, his ideas have had a profound impact on education, emphasizing the importance of active learning, hands-on experiences, and exploration to foster cognitive development. Educators often design activities that encourage students to actively engage with and manipulate their environment to construct their understanding (Rabindran & Madanagopal, 2020).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"Piaget's Stages of Cognitive Development","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Piaget's stages of cognitive development suggest that children go through four distinct stages of cognitive development, each characterized by specific ways of thinking and understanding the world. The stages are sensorimotor, preoperational, concrete operational, and formal operational (Pakpahan & Saragih, 2022). Each stage builds on the previous one. Let’s take a closer look at the stages.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"Piaget and the Sensorimotor Stage","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"The sensorimotor stage is the first stage of children’s cognitive development and spans from birth to 2 years old. During this stage, infants and toddlers learn about the world through their senses and motor actions. They experiment with their bodies and the environment, and they gradually develop a basic understanding of object permanence (more about this later), cause and effect, and stranger anxiety (Malik & Marwaha, 2023).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"Piaget and the Preoperational Stage","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"This stage is from 2 to 7 years old. Children develop symbolic thought, which allows them to represent the world in their minds using words and images. However, their thinking is still egocentric, meaning that they have difficulty seeing things from other people's perspectives (Malik & Marwaha, 2023). We’ll talk more about this when we describe Piaget’s theory of mind.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"Piaget and the Concrete Operational Stage","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"The operational stage is from 7 to 11 years old. Children develop the ability to think logically about concrete objects and events. They can classify objects and understand simple relationships, such as cause and effect (Malik & Marwaha, 2023).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"Piaget and the Formal Operational Stage","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"This stage occurs from age 11 until adulthood. At this stage, children develop the ability to think abstractly and hypothetically. They can reason about things that are not present in the real world and solve complex problems (Malik & Marwaha, 2023).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"In Sum","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Piaget’s theory of cognitive development has had a profound impact on the way we understand how children think and learn. His work has helped educators develop teaching methods that are more effective for different ages and stages of development.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"While Piaget's theory has been highly influential, it has also been criticized for its emphasis on stages and its lack of attention to individual differences. Critics argue that children's cognitive development is not as linear as Piaget suggests and that children may exhibit characteristics of more than one stage at a time.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Despite these criticisms, Piaget's theory remains an important contribution to our understanding of cognitive development. His work has helped us to appreciate the complexity of child development and the important role that interaction plays in learning.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"References","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"li","x":"Malik, F., & Marwaha., R. (2023, April 23). Cognitive development. StatPearls. https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537095/","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Miller, P. H. (2011). Piaget's theory: Past, present, and future. In U. Goswami (Ed.), The Wiley-Blackwell handbook of childhood cognitive development (pp. 649–672. Wiley Blackwell.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Pakpahan, F. H., & Saragih, M. (2022). Theory of cognitive development by Jean Piaget. Journal of Applied Linguistics, 2(1), 55–60.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Rabindran, M. D., & Madanagopal, D. (2020). Piaget’s theory and stages of cognitive development-An overview. Scholars Journal of Applied Medical Sciences, 8(9), 2152–2157.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Scott H. K., & Cogburn M. (2023, January 9). Piaget. StatPearls. https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK448206/","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false}],"read":"4 min read","num":"09"},{"title":"Neuroscience in a Nutshell","dek":"Neuroscience allows us to understand ourselves like never before. Read on to learn about the history and practice of neuroscience.","cat":"brain","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Neuroscience is the scientific study of the nervous system. The brain is, of course, a central focus of neuroscience, but neuroscientific research also includes the study of other parts of the nervous system such as the spinal cord and peripheral nerves, or the nerves that extend throughout our bodies. There are many different levels at which to study the nervous system, from the microscopic study of individual neurons to the macroscopic study of the relationship between brain function and behavior.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Neuroscience is important because it provides the opportunity to improve human life in several ways. Perhaps most obvious is the use of neuroscientific findings to create treatments for diseases and disorders of the nervous system such as Parkinson’s Disease, epilepsy, and spinal cord injuries. Since the early days of brain research, neuroscientists have made amazing strides in creating tools that can greatly reduce the suffering associated with these disorders. For example, in recent years, neuroscientists have created a device that allows people with paraplegia following a spinal cord injury to operate robotic limbs with their minds (Donoghue et al., 2007). While there is still a long way to go before this technology will be easily accessible to everyone, it is nevertheless quite an impressive feat.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Another important function of neuroscience is to provide a window into who we are and how we operate. It allows us to understand our thoughts and behaviors in an objective way and helps us determine how we can best improve important psychological qualities like our mental health.It can also be seen as a way to honor and stand in awe of our complexity as life forms that evolved over billions of years from a primordial soup. Millions of years worth of chance mutations and the will to survive created the most complex system in the universe. And now, that complex system is capable of investigating itself, which is truly amazing.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Additionally, as with any field of science, the pursuit of knowledge can be beneficial in ways we might not anticipate. For example, the discovery of the X-rays, Penicillin, and the anti-malarial drug, Quinine, were all unintentionally discovered by scientists (Krock, 2001). We never know what life-altering benefits neuroscience may provide in the future.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"History of Neuroscience","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"The history of neuroscience can be traced back to the beginning of Greek civilization (Crivellato & Ribatti, 2007). Questions about the seat of the soul and the basis of human thought and rationality posed by ancient Greek philosophers like Socrates, Plato, and Aristotle represent some of the earliest neuroscientific inquiries. Many of these questions are still being explored by neuroscientists today.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"There are many influential figures and notable shifts in understanding in the history of neuroscience between the 6th century B.C.E and today, but one of the most important is Santiago Ramón y Cajal. Ramón y Cajal was a Spanish neuroscientist who is commonly referred to as the father of modern neuroscience (DeFelipe, 2002). This title is principally owed to his work on visualizing the structure of the brain. Using a technique called Golgi staining, Ramón y Cajal was able to identify individual neurons and their complex networks for the first time in unprecedented detail. His work proved that the nervous system was made up of individual cells called neurons, which communicate with each other through specialized structures called synapses.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Ramón y Cajal is also known for his contributions to our understanding of brain development and neuronal plasticity, or the brain's ability to change its structure and function in response to learning and experience. In recognition of his contributions to neuroscience, he was awarded the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 1906, which he shared with the Italian histologist Camillo Golgi. His work continues to influence the field of neuroscience today.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"In Sum","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Neuroscience is a fascinating field of research that has - and will continue to - improve the quality of our lives. In addition to the medical advances it contributes to, neuroscience is a way to understand ourselves better. It is a way for the brain to know itself.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"References","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"li","x":"Crivellato, E., & Ribatti, D. (2007). Soul, mind, brain: Greek philosophy and the birth of neuroscience. Brain research bulletin, 71(4), 327-336.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"DeFelipe, J. (2002). Sesquicentenary of the birthday of Santiago Ramón y Cajal, the father of modern neuroscience. TRENDS in Neurosciences, 25(9), 481-484.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Donoghue, J. P., Nurmikko, A., Black, M., & Hochberg, L. R. (2007). Assistive technology and robotic control using motor cortex ensemble‐based neural interface systems in humans with tetraplegia. The Journal of physiology, 579(3), 603-611.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"li","x":"Krock, L. (2001, February 26). Accidental Discoveries. PBS.org. Retrieved March 26, 2023.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Wendi Lev, LCSW, ACSW, CADC 4/13/2025","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true}],"read":"4 min read","num":"10"},{"title":"Signs That You May Be in a Toxic Relationship","dek":"Discover how toxic relationships come to pass, what they look like, and some ways to reduce the toxicity of your relationships.","cat":"relationships","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Toxic relationships are defined by two chief characteristics: power imbalances and cycling back and forth between good and bad treatment (Dutton & Painter, 1981). Let’s break those two components down a bit.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Would you tolerate a terrible relationship if you had the power to easily walk away from it? Many of us wouldn’t. Toxic relationships can sometimes persist because there is a power imbalance. The person causing harm to the other must have some leverage in the relationship, something that the victimized person wants. This could be money, shelter, or work, but it could also just be love or social connection--basic needs which support our well-being.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"In some toxic relationships, this works both ways, with each person having more power in at least one domain of the relationship. For example, a man might stay with his wife because he has no other source of emotional support, even though she is also emotionally abusive. At the same time, she might stay with him, even though he is physically abusive because otherwise, she will have no financial support.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"This example leads us to the other aspect of toxic relationships: not every part of the relationship is bad because an entirely bad relationship would never reward you for staying in it. In a toxic relationship, the person you’re relating to, whether it’s a friend, family member, or parent, is not just physically, sexually, or emotionally abusive or neglectful; they are also sometimes caring, attentive, generous, and even self-sacrificing.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"This causes what has been called “traumatic bonding” (Dutton & Painter, 1981). At the same time that people in toxic relationships may be traumatized by their experiences in the relationship, they are also emotionally attached to each other because of that trauma. This can make it hard to leave, or change, these relationships.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"What a Toxic Relationship Looks Like","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"In all relationships, people move toward and away from each other, depending on a variety of factors. For example, in a healthy relationship, you might move away from your partner when you feel the need for independence, but you might move toward them when you feel anxious or when you feel like you need to have a conversation about the relationship.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"In toxic relationships, people move toward and away from each other in ineffective or damaging ways; they also make moves to get “one up” on the other person, or to put the other person “one down” (McLemore, 2003).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"This can look a variety of ways (McLemore, 2003). For example, moving toward somebody can look like controlling, engulfing, or attacking the other person. Meanwhile, moving away can look like avoiding, freeloading, giving them the silent treatment, or ghosting them.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"What Causes Toxic Relationships?","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"A helpful way to understand how toxic relationships form is to understand the impacts of not getting attachment needs met. Attachment theory tells us that how people relate to each other as adults relies in large part on how they did (or didn’t) get their needs met as children (Hazan & Shaver, 1987). If we learn harmful but effective ways of getting our needs met as children, we tend to carry some form of those behaviors into adulthood (Bartholomew, 1997). Typically, these behaviors revolve around making sure we get the care that we need, often through controlling others (Reder & Duncan, 2001).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"That makes it sound like one person is the problem, right? Actually, abusive relationships generally only persist when two people who have unhealthy ways of relating connect with each other (Motz, 2014). This may be the case between a parent and child, where the child has learned unhealthy attachment behaviors from the parent, or it may happen between two adults who separately learned these harmful, even abusive behaviors. A person with a secure attachment style will not stick around and tolerate abusive behaviors; they know that they deserve better. But a person who has learned their own unhealthy ways of relating is more likely to get stuck in trying to make a toxic relationship work.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Tragically, these toxic ways of relating are often modeled by parents, internalized by their children, and reenacted by those children when they grow up (Motz, 2014). It is hard for children to witness these behaviors and not come to learn and identify with those behaviors. And since these behaviors tend to be learned from the people who love them, children learn to associate them with love, to the point that toxic ways of relating start to seem like loving acts.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"This also helps explain why so many people linger in toxic relationships. In fact, the more emotionally attached we are to people who abuse us, the more likely we are to come back to that relationship again and again, despite the negative consequences (Griffing et al., 2002).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"How To Move On","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"A key way to break out of toxic relationship patterns is to reject power dynamics that cause one person to be above or below the other (McLemore, 2003). What would this look like? This looks like shifting from disengaging and besting the other person to attaching to and caring for the other person (McLemore, 2003).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"For example, disengaging can look like stonewalling somebody, or simply giving in to something you don’t want. Instead, try to let them in, but not too much. If your romantic partner wants to go out dancing tonight, and you’re not in the mood, engage with them while holding boundaries. It might look like saying, “I appreciate that you want to spend time together. I want that, too, but I don’t have the energy for dancing. What else would be fun for us to do tonight?”","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"In Sum","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"It’s important to differentiate between times when we do something unhealthy in our relationships versus relationships that are themselves toxic. We all sometimes act on negative feelings in unhelpful ways. But if a relationship feels that way often – if it seems like you and the other person really bring out the worst in each other, and you’re not sure how to fix it – we hope you find your way to some help. Sympathetic friends, family members, and even therapists are waiting to support you in reducing the toxicity of your relationships.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"References","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Bartholomew, K. (1997). Adult attachment processes: individual and couple perspectives. British Journal of Medical Psychology, 70, 249-263.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Dutton, D. G., & Painter, S. L. (1981). Traumatic bonding: The development of emotional attachments in battered women and other relationships of intermittent abuse. Victimology: An International Journal, 7(4), 139-155.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Griffing, S., Ragin, D. F., Sage, R. E., Madry, L., Bingham, L. E., & Primm, B. J. (2002). Domestic violence survivors' self-identified reasons for returning to abusive relationships. Journal of Interpersonal Violence, 17(3), 306-319.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"li","x":"Hazan, C., & Shaver, P. R. (1987). Romantic love conceptualized as an attachment process. Journal of Personality and Social Psychology, 52, 511-524.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"McLemore, C. W. (2003). Toxic relationships and how to change them: health and holiness in everyday life. San Francisco: Jossey-Bass.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Motz, A. (2014). Toxic couples: the psychology of domestic violence. Routledge.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Reder, P., & Duncan, S. (2001). Abusive relationships, care and control conflicts and insecure attachments. Child Abuse Review, 10, 411-427.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false}],"read":"6 min read","num":"11"},{"title":"Understand Yourself Better Through the Work of Carl Jung","dek":"Learn Carl Jung’s theories about the mysterious self.","cat":"self","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Carl Jung (1875–1961) was a Swiss psychiatrist and psychoanalyst who founded analytical psychology. Throughout his career he introduced several concepts that helped shape the development of modern psychology. For example, he developed theories about a set of universal experiences he believed to be shared by all humans as well as the symbols and motifs that tend to recur in myths and dreams. He also developed many of our current concepts relating to personality including introversion, extroversion, and individuation—the process of becoming one’s true self (Fordham & Fordham, 2023).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Part of what makes Jung such a significant figure in modern psychology is the depth with which he explored the human psyche. Like his contemporary Sigmund Freud, he believed there was much to be discovered beneath the veil of the conscious mind. The impact of his work and ideas extended far beyond psychology and into fields like literature, art, and religion. Let’s talk more about some of his most profound and enduring ideas like archetypes, synchronicity, and the shadow self (Fordham & Fordham, 2023).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"Carl Jung’s Contributions to Psychology","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Jung was a prolific thinker and made many contributions to the field of psychology. Below are a few of his most well-known contributions (Darowski & Darowski, 2016; Leigh, 2011).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Analytical Psychology","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Jung developed his own school of psychology known as analytical psychology. It differs from Freudian psychoanalysis in its emphasis on the exploration of the unconscious mind and the integration of different elements of the psyche.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Collective Unconscious","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Jung introduced the concept of the collective unconscious, suggesting that there are universal symbols and themes shared by all humans across cultures. Archetypes, such as the hero, mother, and shadow, are examples of these universal symbols.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Archetypes","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Jung identified archetypes as recurring symbols or motifs in myths, dreams, and art. These archetypes are considered to be innate and shared by all individuals, representing fundamental human experiences and emotions.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Personality Types","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Jung was an early pioneer of the idea of personality types. He identified dichotomies such as introversion vs. extraversion, thinking vs. feeling, sensing vs. intuition, and judging vs. perceiving. The dichotomies are now the foundation of modern personality tests such as the Myers-Briggs Type Indicator.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Individuation","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Jung emphasized the process of individuation, which involves the integration of various aspects of the self to achieve a balanced and whole personality. It includes the exploration and acceptance of both conscious and unconscious elements.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Complex Theory","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Jung introduced the concept of complexes, which are emotionally charged groups of ideas or images. Complexes can influence an individual's thoughts, feelings, and behaviors, often leading to internal conflicts. One well-known example of Jung’s complex theory is the inferiority complex.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Dream Analysis","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Jung placed significant importance on dream analysis as a means of exploring the unconscious. He believed that dreams contain symbolic messages that can provide insights into an individual's psychological state and personal growth.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"More On Archetypes","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Jung defined archetypes as fundamental, universal symbols or themes that emerge from the collective unconscious and are shared by all human beings. Each archetype is an embodiment of recurring patterns and motifs found in myths, dreams, and cultural expressions across different times and civilizations. They were largely explored and refined through careful study of a multitude of works of literature, poetry, and religious traditions (Leigh, 2011). Each archetype encompasses specific characteristics and meanings that tap into deep, innate aspects of the human psyche.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Jung states, “There are as many archetypes as there are typical situations in life” (Jung, 1936). However, there were a few that seemed to dominate literature, myths, and dreams. Below are some of the archetypes Jung identified through his studies.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"The Hero","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"The hero archetype represents the individual's journey to overcome challenges, face adversity, and achieve personal growth. Heroes are often depicted as figures who embark on a quest or face a significant task.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"The Shadow","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"The shadow archetype encompasses the darker, unconscious aspects of the self. It includes repressed desires, instincts, and aspects of personality that an individual may find difficult to acknowledge. Integrating the shadow is crucial for personal development.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"The Anima and Animus","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"The anima represents the feminine aspects within the male psyche, while the animus represents the masculine aspects within the female psyche. These archetypes reflect the idea that each individual contains elements of both genders.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"The Persona","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"The persona is the social mask or facade that an individual presents to the outside world. It represents the way a person wants to be perceived by others and may not necessarily reflect the true self.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"The Self","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"The self is the archetype representing the integration of all aspects of the individual's personality. It is the goal of individuation, where the conscious and unconscious elements come together to form a balanced and whole self.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"The Mother","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"The mother archetype symbolizes nurturing, protection, and fertility. It is often associated with themes of motherhood, caregiving, and the life-giving aspects of the feminine.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"The Father","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"The father archetype represents authority, guidance, and protection. It embodies qualities of leadership, structure, and discipline. The father archetype is associated with the paternal or authoritative aspects of the masculine.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"The Child","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"The child archetype embodies innocence, potential, and the promise of new beginnings. It represents the capacity for creativity, spontaneity, and a sense of wonder.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"The Wise Old Man/Woman","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"This archetype represents wisdom, guidance, and the accumulation of life experiences. It is often depicted as an elderly figure who imparts knowledge and serves as a mentor.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"In Sum","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Carl Jung’s thoughts and ideas shaped modern psychology, and his influence on fields like literature and art is still apparent today. Some of his most influential concepts include archetypes, the collective unconscious, and the Jungian shadow. These concepts and others helped open a new door into potential functions of the human mind and different perspectives from which we can begin to make sense of our human experience.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"References","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Darowski, E. S. and Darowski, J. J. (2016). Carl Jung's historic place in psychology and continuing influence in narrative studies and American popular culture. Swiss American Historical Society Review, 52(2), 2.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"li","x":"Fordham, M. S. M. and Fordham, F. (2023). Carl Jung. Encyclopedia Britannica. https://www.britannica.com/biography/Carl-Jung","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Jung, C. G. (1936). The concept of the collective unconscious. Collected works, 9(1), 42.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false}],"read":"5 min read","num":"12"},{"title":"Understanding Fear and the Amygdala","dek":"This brain region drives our experiences of fear, among other things.","cat":"brain","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Close to two hundred years ago, a doctor dissecting a human body first recognized the region of the brain that would come to be known as the amygdala (Ledoux, 2007). Since this region was shaped like an almond, and about the same size, too, it was named after the Latin word for almond, which is, of course, amygdala.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"It was originally thought that the amygdala was a standalone region of the brain, but researchers soon observed that it had closely related and nearby structures that seemed to be involved in the same activities. After this, a fierce debate raged about which parts of the brain were close enough in proximity and in function to count as part of the amygdala.That scholars eventually agreed to call the original almond-like shape, as well as some other adjacent brain parts engaged in very similar activities, the “amygdaloid complex”, or just ‘amygdala’ for short.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"So to summarize, the definition of the amygdala is a tiny brain region that is highly involved in certain emotional and cognitive processes – more on those in a moment. This region is a very evolutionarily old part of the brain—it is involved in very “primitive” functions such as our sense of smell, but it is also highly interconnected with evolutionarily newer parts of the brain (Swanson & Petrovich, 1998).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"Function of the Amygdala","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"The function for which the amygdala is best known is its role in fear conditioning (Janak & Tye, 2015). This means that it is highly involved in both the experience of feeling fear and the experience of learning to fear something. When we have an experience that frightens us, our amygdalae (that’s the plural of the word!) help the more complex regions of the brain, such as our prefrontal cortex, encode (a fancy word for remembering) this experience as something we should be afraid of.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"While fear conditioning is the best studied of the amygdala’s many functions (Ledoux, 2007), the amygdala is involved in many other emotion-related processes (Janak & Tye, 2015). It has become clear that the amygdala also fires – in other words, shows a lot of activity – when we have positive emotions as well as negative emotions, and when we see things that are rewarding as well as threatening (Baxter & Murray, 2002).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"In other words, when we are motivated to move toward something or away from it, the amygdala is functioning as part of that process (Janak & Tye, 2015). It is also involved in our behaviors that seek to meet basic needs, such as seeking comfort, eating, and sexual activity (Ledoux, 2007). It even is activated in accordance with what we are paying attention to. So one shorthand that has been proposed is that the amygdala’s job is to notice when things are emotionally relevant to us (Ledoux, 2007).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"The Amygdala and PTSD","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"It appears that the amygdala is significantly changed in people who have posttraumatic stress disorder, or PTSD (Morey et al., 2012). The amygdala of a person with PTSD shows more activity in response to emotion-inducing images than the amygdala of a person without PTSD. People with PTSD have smaller amygdalae on average, too. At the same time, these phenomena are not worse in people with worse PTSD symptoms. This suggests that people with PTSD may have been more susceptible to strong reactions to stressful events in the first place.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"The Amygdala and Anxiety","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Since anxiety and fear are somewhat synonymous, it should not surprise us that amygdalae look different in people with anxiety. Similar to the amygdalae of people with PTSD, the amygdala of an anxious person is likely to be smaller and more reactive than the amygdala of a person without anxiety (Davis, 1992; Rauch et al., 2003).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"In Sum","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Perhaps you have read articles online or talked to friends or even healthcare professionals about what you can do to manage your amygdala. This is an admirable goal, but perhaps not the most effective way to think about how to get there. The absence of fear would bring us all sorts of very real, new problems. Also, we do not experience less fear by changing our brains – we change our brains by having new experiences.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Psychotherapy is where we truly, although indirectly, work on the amygdala. Fear is the belief that something bad will happen, but every time we face something frightening in therapy, we break down that association a little bit. The amygdala’s response may just weaken a bit (Janak & Tye, 2015), creating more freedom in our lives. Hopefully you can gently and courageously face the fears you’ve been conditioned to have but that are holding you back.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"References","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Baxter, M. G., & Murray, E. A. (2002). The amygdala revisited. Nature Reviews Neuroscience, 3, 563-573.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Davis, M. (1992). The role of the amygdala in fear and anxiety. Annual Review of Neuroscience, 15, 353-375.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Janak, P. H. & Tye, K. M. (2015). From circuits to behavior in the amygdala. Nature, 517, 284-292.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Ledoux, J. (2007). The amygdala. Current Biology, 17(20), R868-R874.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Morey, R. A., Gold, A. L., LaBar, K. S., Beall, S. K., Brown, V. M., …, & McCarthy, G. (2012). Amygdala volume changes in posttraumatic stress disorder in a large case-controlled veterans group. Archives of General Psychiatry, 69(11), 1169-1178.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Rauch, S. L., Shin, L. M., & Wright, C. I. (2003). Neuroimaging studies of amygdala function in anxiety disorders. Annals of the New York Academy of Sciences, 985(1), 389-410.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"li","x":"Swanson, L. W., & Petrovich, G. D. (1998). What is the amygdala? Trends in Neurosciences, 21(8), 323-331.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false}],"read":"4 min read","num":"13"},{"title":"What Is Blunted Affect?","dek":"Learn about the symptoms, causes, and experience of blunted affect.","cat":"emotions","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"We usually take notice when somebody isn’t expressing themselves and their emotions in the ways we typically expect. Although everybody has days when their emotions are higher or lower, people who are experiencing blunted affect stand out because they rarely or never express emotion.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Affect is a word psychologists use for the expression of emotions, and blunted, in this case, means dulled, weakened, or slowed down. Typically, this means minimal demonstration of emotion through facial expressions, making less eye contact in conversation, using fewer gestures and other forms of body language to express emotion, and having a flat tone of voice (Padmanabhan & Keshavan, 2016).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Psychologists and psychiatrists came up with the definition of blunted affect through careful observation of people experiencing mental health challenges. Specifically, we know from research that blunted affect is common in several groups of psychiatric disorders, including schizophrenia and its related disorders, bipolar disorder, depressive and anxiety disorders, post-traumatic stress disorder, and autism spectrum disorder (Strauss & Cohen, 2017).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Although blunted affect may look much the same on the outside, the experience of blunted affect on the inside looks different across these disorders. For some people, blunted affect reflects the fact that they are not experiencing much emotion—they feel numb or empty inside. Other people with blunted affect are experiencing a typical range of emotions, but they are unable to physically demonstrate those emotions in the ways we usually expect.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"On the outside, blunted affect may involve speech that is monotone or robotic, where the words you would expect to hear emphasized are not. A person with blunted affect will also respond without emotion to events that would evoke emotions in most people, such as learning that one received a promotion or that a loved one was in a car accident. People experiencing blunted affect say that it is even hard for them to force their faces into emotional expressions (Ekman, 2003; Gabay et al., 2015).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"As you might imagine, being on either side of the equation in a conversation where somebody has blunted affect can be difficult. Human connection relies on the ability to empathize, to feel not only our own emotions but also intuit and feel what others are feeling (Fredrickson, 2013). People with blunted affect have more difficulty doing this (Lee et al., 2014), making it harder for them to build and maintain relationships.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"What Causes Blunted Affect?","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Brain imaging studies have shown that the parts of the brain responsible for paying attention to emotions, recognizing facial expressions, and helping us empathize and imagine what other people are thinking, are functioning worse in people with blunted affect (Chuang et al., 2014; Stoodley & Schmahmann, 2010). These findings have led some scientists to believe that impairments in thinking (for example, not recognizing one’s own emotions) are one of the causes of blunted affect (Boden et al., 2021; Strauss & Cohen, 2017).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Similarly, other research suggests that blunted affect may also be caused by people’s brains not recognizing cues of emotions, perhaps because their mirror neurons—the parts of our brain that make us feel what another person is feeling—are less active when they are interacting with other people (Lee et al., 2014). However, some research suggests that people with blunted affect only have trouble expressing emotion, not feeling it, which means they may recognize other people’s feelings but not be able to respond to them effectively (Aghevli et al., 2003).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"In summary, blunted affect is probably caused by a breakdown in the links between the parts of the brain involved in emotions and other parts of the brain, such as the prefrontal cortex (where thinking and decision-making primarily happen) and the motor cortex (which controls physical action).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"References","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Aghevli, M. A., Blanchard, J. J., & Horan, W. P. (2003). The expression and experience of emotion in schizophrenia: a study of social emotions. Psychiatry Research, 119(3), 261-270.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Boden, R., Bengtsson, J., Thornblom, E., Struckmann, W., & Persson, J. (2021). Dorsomedial prefrontal theta burst stimulation to treat anhedonia, avolition, and blunted affect in schizophrenia or depression – a randomized controlled trial. Journal of Affective Disorders, 290, 308-315.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Chuang, J. Y., Murray, G. K., Metastasio, A., Segarra, N., Tait, R., …, & Suckling, J. (2014). Brain structural signatures of negative symptoms in depression and schizophrenia. Frontiers in Psychiatry, 5, 116.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"li","x":"Ekman, P. (2003). Darwin, deception, and facial expression. Annals of the New York Academy of Sciences, 1000, 205-221.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Fredrickson, B. (2013). Love 2.0: How our supreme emotion affects everything we think, do, feel, and become. Random House.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Gabay, A. S., Kempton, M. J., & Mehta, M. A. (2015). Facial affect processing deficits in schizophrenia: a meta-analysis of antipsychotic treatment effects. Journal of Psychopharmacology, 29(2), 224-229.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Lee, J. S., Chun, J. W., Yoon, S. Y., Park, H., & Kim, J. (2014). Involvement of the mirror neuron system in blunted affect in schizophrenia. Schizophrenia Research, 152(1), 268-274.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"li","x":"Padmanabhan, J. L., & Keshavan, M. S. (2016). Schizophrenia. In Howard S. Friedman (Ed.), Encyclopedia of Mental Health (pp. 55-65). Academic Press.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Stoodley, C. J., & Schmahmann, J. D. (2010). Evidence for topographic organization in the cerebellum of motor control versus cognitive and affective processing. Cortex, 46, 831-844.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"li","x":"Strauss, G. P., & Cohen, A. S. (2017). A transdiagnostic review of negative symptom phenomenology and etiology. Schizophrenia Bulletin, 43(4), 712-719","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Wendi Lev, LCSW, ACSW, CADC 4/3/25","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true}],"read":"4 min read","num":"14"},{"title":"What Is Emotional Lability?","dek":"Here’s what science says about causes, symptoms, and treatment of emotional lability.","cat":"emotions","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Do you feel like your emotions swing all over the place? Once you start experiencing emotions, does it feel hard to stop? Or, do your emotions feel especially intense? Then you may be experiencing some form of emotional lability.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Emotional lability is a rapid and intense change in a person’s emotions or mood, typically inappropriate to the setting (Posner et al., 2014). For example, a person can suddenly start crying uncontrollably because of any strong emotion, even though they do not feel sad, frustrated, or happy. The term originally comes from the Latin word labilis, meaning “transient, fleeting, slippery” (World of Dictionary, n.d.), which highlights the dramatic and frequent shift between emotional experiences. Emotional lability can also be an over-expression of positive emotions, such as enthusiasm or energy, either disproportionate to the event or improper to the circumstance (Posner et al., 2014). One example is when a person suddenly laughs uncontrollably at a funeral.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, 5th Edition (DSM-5; American Psychiatric Association, 2013) says that emotional lability appears as a symptom in various disorders, including:","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"li","x":"Borderline personality disorder (BPD), which is a mental illness characterized by unstable moods, impulsive behavior, and problems in relationships.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Bipolar disorder (BP), which is characterized by episodes of intense mood swings, with depressive lows and manic highs. In Bipolar disorder, emotional lability often lasts longer (e.g., days-weeks; Posner et al., 2014)","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"li","x":"Substance use, which can include alcohol or any illicit drugs.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD), a common psychiatric disorder defined by inattention, hyperactivity, and impulsivity.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD), a disorder in which a person struggles to recover after witnessing or experiencing a traumatic event.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"h3","x":"What Are Emotional Lability Symptoms?","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Extreme emotions and sudden mood shifts. For example, those suffering from emotional lability experience intense feelings that change rapidly and often fluctuate through the day.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"li","x":"Improper reactions. If a person laughs uncontrollably at a funeral, it is a sign of emotional lability, as it refers to inappropriate reactions.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Difficulty controlling emotions. People who experience emotional lability may have little control of their feelings, which can make it more difficult to stop them.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Emotional lability can negatively impact a person’s daily life and these behaviors can be confusing or embarrassment","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Be aware. It is very important to be aware of what triggers emotional lability and to try to avoid them whenever possible. Some of these triggers include extreme fatigue, stress, or certain social situations or environments.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Take care of yourself. By taking care of yourself, both physically and mentally, you may reduce tension and stress. Make sure that you get enough sleep every night, have a balanced diet, and exercise regularly. By doing something that makes you feel better, such as going on a stroll or spending quality time alone, you may increase your overall well-being.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"li","x":"Take a break. If you experience lability, it’s ok to take a step back for a few minutes to cope with the emotions.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Find a distraction. Sometimes it can be helpful to change the topic or to engage in a different activity to reduce stimulation and regain control of the emotions.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Acknowledge the feelings. It’s important to realize that sometimes you can’t control your emotions, and that’s OK. You just have to take care of yourself and practice self-compassion.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"li","x":"Educate. Other people can be confused or frightened about what they can’t understand. If you feel comfortable, you can let people know what happened.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Ask for help. It is important to remember that you are not alone in this. You can talk to your friends or family members, or ask for help from a professional, such as a therapist or psychiatrist.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"In summary, emotional lability can be a challenge. By understanding its roots, it may become easier to manage.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"References","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Acquired Brain Injury Outreach Service (2021). Understanding Emotional Lability.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"American Psychiatric Association. (2013). Diagnostic and statistical manual of mental disorders (5th ed.).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Posner, J., Kass, E., & Hulvershorn, L. (2014). Using stimulants to treat ADHD-related emotional lability. Current psychiatry reports, 16(10), 478.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"World of Dictionary. (n.d.). Labilis. Retrieved November 15, 2021.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false}],"read":"3 min read","num":"15"},{"title":"What Is Gaslighting and How to Recognize It?","dek":"Discover signs and examples of gaslighting, as well as suggestions for stopping it.","cat":"relationships","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"The term gaslighting comes from the 1944 movie about a newly married couple called Gaslight. In the film, the husband tries to drive his new wife crazy so that he can claim her wealth for himself. His primary tactic for doing this is to turn up and down the brightness of the gaslights in their apartment, denying all the while that anything is changing. This is just one of several actions he takes to make her start to doubt her own senses and perceptions; eventually, she accepts reality as he tells her it is, instead.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Gaslighting is engaging in behaviors that make the world surreal for somebody else, making them question their own reality (Ferraro, 2006). This may be achieved by manipulating somebody’s perceptions, memory, reasoning, or emotions (Sodoma, 2022). And the ultimate outcome of gaslighting is typically a person who cannot disagree with the gaslighter because they no longer believe in any of their own thoughts, feelings, or perceptions (Abramson, 2014). However, in the movie example, the husband is quite aware of what he is doing. In real life, not all gaslighters are conscious of their actions – not that this excuses their behavior.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Phrases that likely indicate someone is trying, intentionally or unintentionally, to gaslight you, include the following:","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"li","x":"“You must not be remembering that right.”","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“I’m not going to argue with you, but I know what I know.”","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“There’s no reason to feel that way.”","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“I sometimes think you’re making these things up just to have an excuse to be mad with me.”","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“You’re always the victim here, aren’t you? It’s always my fault with you.”","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“I can’t believe you feel that way – it just doesn’t make sense.”","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"“I didn’t do anything wrong. You’re just too sensitive.”","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Another way to identify examples of gaslighting is to think about how you might go about eroding somebody’s sense of reality. Efforts to hide important details, lie about one’s own actions, and control the narrative and the person in general, are all behaviors that can have a gaslighting effect (Petric, 2022).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Suppose your work colleague fails to meet a deadline in a way that impacts your work together. When you ask them about the situation, gaslighting responses might look like some of the following (Petric, 2022):","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"li","x":"Lying: “I did send you that report. Maybe you should check your email more closely.”","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Misdirection: “I’m pretty sure somebody else on the team was supposed to do that. And shouldn’t you remember who you asked to do this?”","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Denial: “There’s no way that was my responsibility. If it was, I would have it on my to-do list, and look, it’s not here.”","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Nonverbal behaviors: If somebody glares, audibly sighs, or rolls their eyes, but vehemently denies they are doing these things, or denies that these behaviors have any meaning behind them, they may be part of a gaslighting pattern.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"Origin of Gaslighting","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Generally, people who are more likely to gaslight others have poor conflict resolution skills and are not good at managing their close relationships (Brodie et al., 2018). They tend to have trouble understanding what they and other people are thinking. In other words, they have a hard time thinking about thinking (Bateman & Fonagy, 2008), which means they are probably not fully aware of what they are doing. This example is not meant to be belittling, but you could perhaps draw a comparison to when a child vigorously denies doing something they clearly have done.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Gaslighting behaviors appear to be done more often by men to women than the other way around (Hester et al., 2017). In fact, some scholars argue that gaslighting based on gender is taking place not just between partners but on a broader cultural and social level, as the experiences of women are routinely discredited and doubted (for example, when women who have experienced sexual assault are disbelieved) (Stark, 2019).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"How To Deal With Gaslighting","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"In a popular book on how to deal with gaslighting, Robin Stern (2018) stressed the importance of the gaslightee taking an active role in breaking the cycle of gaslighting. (After all, what incentive does the gaslighter have to stop their behavior?)","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Stern suggests that if you are being gaslighted, ask yourself why you want the approval of the gaslighting person. Once you have identified the reason, you can ask yourself whether that is a healthy expectation. If it is not, you may want to write down what healthy boundaries in the relationship would look like. Then, practice a more effective response pattern – one that does not engage the gaslighter’s version of events and that validates and protects your own needs. It may be helpful to roleplay this kind of situation with a good friend or even a therapist.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"In Sum","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Gaslighting has gotten a lot of attention in the press and social media recently, and for good reason; it’s an unfortunately common and harmful way that people treat each other. Hopefully, you now feel better able to recognize gaslighting when it happens and respond effectively to it, including if you yourself have gaslighted someone before. People who gaslight can learn to get their relationship needs met in healthier ways, and people who are gaslighted can draw boundaries to keep themselves from being abused in relationships. We all deserve healthy and happy relationships with healthy and happy boundaries.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"References","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Abramson, K. (2014). Turning up the lights on gaslighting. Philosophical Perspectives, 28(1), 1-30.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Bateman, A., & Fonagy, P. (2008). 8-year follow-up of patients treated for borderline personality disorder: Mentalization based treatment versus treatment as usual. The American Journal of Psychiatry, 165(5), 631–638.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Brodie, Z. P., Goodall, K., Darling, S., & McVittie, C. (2018). Attachment insecurity and dispositional aggression: The mediating role of maladaptive anger regulation. Journal of Social and Personal Relationships, 36(6), 1831–1852.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"li","x":"Ferraro, K. J. (2006). Neither angels nor demons: women, crime, and victimization. Boston: Northeastern University Press.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Hester, M., Jones, C., Williamson, E., Fahmy, E., & Feder, G. (2017). Is it coercive controlling violence? A cross-sectional domestic violence and abuse survey of men attending general practice in England. Psychology of Violence, 7(3), 417–27.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"li","x":"Petric, D. (2022). Psychology of abusive human behavior. Open Journal of Medical Psychology, 11(2), 29-38.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Stark, C. A. (2019). Gaslighting, misogyny, and psychological oppression. The Monist, 102(2), 221-235.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Stern, R. (2018). The gaslight effect: How to spot and survive the hidden manipulation others use to control your life. New York: Harmony Books.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false}],"read":"5 min read","num":"16"},{"title":"What Is Minimizing and How To Stop Doing It","dek":"Learn about the underlying theory of minimization, discover its causes, and explore tips to stop it.","cat":"communication","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Why do we minimize something remarkable as if it is not worthy of praise or discussion? In this article, we’ll define minimizing, discuss why we minimize our experiences and events, and explore what we can do to stop minimizing.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"What Is Minimizing in Psychology?","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"American Psychological Association (APA) defines minimizing as a “cognitive distortion consisting of a tendency to present events to oneself or others as insignificant or unimportant” (APA, 2022). A cognitive distortion alters reality in our minds, making something out of nothing, or, in this case, making nothing out of something. In other words, minimizing is when we frame something to be lesser than it is by denying or dismissing its significance.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"Examples of Minimizing","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Minimizing is quite common, and many of us minimize our experiences, emotions, or the effects of events on us. Moreover, we minimize both the good and the bad. Here are a few examples that you might find in daily life.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Manuel was standing between two isles in a convenience store when a robber barged in. The robber held the store employee at gunpoint as he emptied the cash register. Manuel carefully approached the robber from behind and knocked him unconscious with a case of soda. Manuel was declared a hero when the police and the local news crews arrived. However, Manuel refused to take credit for his bravery and declared that anyone would do the same.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Albert was recently promoted at work. At a party, many people congratulated him and commented that he’s always been a hard worker with high standards. Albert shrugged off these comments and insisted that his supervisors must have confused him with someone else who actually deserved to be promoted.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Carmen attended a family event a few weeks after her miscarriage, where her relatives kept telling her how sorry they were. Carmen sighed and told them that it was early in the pregnancy anyway, and her situation was nothing major compared to other parents who have lost their children to cancer or other chronic conditions.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"How To Stop Minimizing","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Although minimizing something every once in a while isn’t necessarily bad, frequent use of minimization may be a sign that you avoid dealing with your emotions. Furthermore, when you always minimize your role in situations, you may appear avoidant, indecisive, or unconfident. The more you minimize the importance of your accomplishments, the more likely you will perpetuate a self-fulfilling prophecy. For instance, when it comes to your career, you might be passed over for promotions or salary increases you actually deserve.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"1. Ask yourself why you minimize. Sometimes we minimize everything as if we are on autopilot because we don’t see ourselves in a positive light. Maybe we avoid dealing with our emotions or are conditioned to appear modest. Once you figure out why you minimize, you can address the root causes. For instance, if you suffer from low-self esteem, you can work on strengthening your perception of your self-worth.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"2. Use positive affirmations. Positive affirmations are supportive statements about ourselves that can shift our mindsets toward positivity. Whether you are conditioned to be modest or lack self-esteem and confidence, positive affirmations such as, “I deserve success” or “I am proud of myself,” can help you see yourself in a more positive light.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"3. Change your responses. You might need to do a spring cleaning and replace the minimizing words and phrases with those that aren’t dismissive. You may say, “thank you for your kind words” instead of “I don’t deserve all these compliments,” or “thanks for noticing my efforts” instead of “I didn’t really do that much.”","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"Final Thoughts on Minimizing","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Minimizing is a cognitive distortion characterized by the tendency to reframe events to reduce their significance. Minimizing can help us cope with situations and emotions that may be hard for us to accept or deal with. We all use minimization once in a while. However, persistent minimization may make us appear avoidant or unconfident. Understanding the underlying causes of why we minimize in certain situations can help us address these causes and develop strategies to stop minimizing.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"References","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"APA (2022). APA Dictionary of Psychology. Retrieved 2 May 2022.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false}],"read":"3 min read","num":"17"},{"title":"What is Psychological Health?","dek":"Find out the different facets of psychological health and the services available to support you.","cat":"wellbeing","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Life’s unique challenges undoubtedly affect us all differently. Maybe you’re going to college and struggling with leaving home for the first time. Perhaps you went through a tough time in life and used alcohol to numb the pain. Or, you dealt with family trauma as a child that led to anxious feelings and intrusive thoughts as an adult.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Whether you’re going through life transitions, struggling with addiction, or just trying to quiet down your mind, no matter where you are in your mental health journey, thank you for being here. Talking about your psychological health can sometimes be as difficult as dealing with the emotions and behaviors that may accompany psychological health concerns. In this article, we hope to not only give you information about how to better understand your psychological health and services that you may consider utilizing but to also validate your journey. Let’s get started.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"What Is Psychological Health?","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"The field of psychological health focuses on emotional, cognitive, behavioral, and social well-being. Like physical health, psychological health is an integral part of our overall holistic wellness. Healthy psychological well-being is not just the absence of signs of mental health issues or a diagnosis of a mental health disorder, but the presence of balanced emotions, thoughts, and behaviors. When our psychological state is unbalanced, we may have trouble with indecisiveness, managing our emotions, controlling our behaviors, interacting with others, and handling stress and other challenges (Galderisi et al., 2015).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"Topics in Psychological Health","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Because psychological health is multifaceted, several topics exist within the field. Here is a list of some issues that are covered under the umbrella term ‘psychological health’ (World Health Organization, 2004).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"li","x":"Anxiety Disorders","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Depressive Moods & Major Depression Disorder","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Bipolar Disorders","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD)","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD)","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder (OCD)","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Post Traumatic Stress Disorder (PTSD)","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Eating Disorders","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Personality Disorders","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Learning Disabilities","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Schizophrenia","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Psychosis","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Alzheimer’s","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Intimate Partner Violence","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Domestic Abuse","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Sexual Assault","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Suicide and Suicidal Ideation","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Grief and Bereavement","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Substance Abuse and Addiction","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Anger Management","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Chronic Illness","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"School Concerns (e.g., bullying)","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Conflict Management","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Self-Esteem","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Self-Harm","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Phobias","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Marital Issues","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Women’s Health","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Men’s Health","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Children’s Health","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"LGBTQ Issues","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Racism and Racial Battle Fatigue","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Body Dysmorphia and Body Image","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Athlete’s Health and Injury","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Rehabilitation","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"HIV/AIDS","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Sexual Disorders","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Personal Growth","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Resilience","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Disabilities","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Communication","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Spirituality","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"The takeaway here is that psychological health can be affected in a myriad of ways, whether it’s mental health concerns, physical health issues, life changes, personal development, community concerns, and/or political and social justice issues. This is the reason why a plethora of different professionals exist to support you through different threats to your psychological health.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"Psychological Health Services and Professionals","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Humans are complex creatures—each of us with our niche life experiences, realities, and struggles. Just as treatment services for physical health concerns remain aplenty, psychological health services are broad too.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Here is a non-exhaustive list of examples of various services and types of professionals working in mental health.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Psychotherapy. Therapists who practice psychotherapy treat mental health conditions such as anxiety, depression, bipolar disorder, eating disorders, addiction, and personality disorders. However, these professionals do not only help their clients manage diagnosed conditions, but can also support individuals going through life changes such as moving, going to college, getting married, stressful situations, grief and bereavement, and conflict resolution. Psychotherapists tend to use various forms of talk therapy and science-based treatment options such as cognitive-behavioral therapy. Different types of psychotherapists include licensed mental health counselors, social workers, counseling psychologists, and clinical psychologists (Lambert, Bergin, & Garfield, 1994).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Psychiatry. While psychotherapists practice a form of counseling, psychiatrists help support various mental health conditions through medication. Several mental health concerns such as depression, panic disorder, anxiety, obsessive-compulsive disorder, attention deficit hyperactivity disorder (ADHD), schizophrenia, substance abuse, and bipolar disorder often use both counseling and medication methods to mitigate symptoms. Psychotherapists most often have their formal clinical and research education in master’s and PhD programs, while psychiatrists attend medical school (Kleinman, 2008).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Marriage and Family Therapy. Similar to psychotherapists, practitioners of marriage and family therapy use counseling primarily for working with people who may be dealing with family issues (e.g., tense parent-child dynamics), spousal concerns (e.g., poor communication), and overall relationship changes (e.g., marriage or divorce). Therapy is often offered for individuals as well as for couples and families (Broderick & Schrader, 1991).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Social Work. Social workers have some commonalities with psychotherapists in their training. However, a big component of social work also includes advocacy of psychological health and social concerns. When they are not providing therapy to their client, many social workers may also be involved with reducing mental health stigma, advocating for psychological health policy changes (e.g., more access to services in lower income communities), and promoting social, cultural, and economic civil rights (Adams, 2003).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"School Psychology. School psychologists support the psychological health of students. These practitioners are often available in school settings as early as preschool to assist students with developmental concerns, learning challenges, and behavioral issues. Their goal is to not only support mental health, but improve student-teacher dynamics and promote academic achievement for all types of student learners (Fagan & Wise, 2000).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Additional Services. Other types of psychological health services that may be available include substance abuse counselors, suicide prevention hotlines, crisis counselors, group therapy, peer educators and specialists, and telehealth or mobile apps for therapy.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"In Sum","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Let’s recap. In this article, we defined what psychological health is, which factors can impact your mental well-being, the different topics the umbrella of psychological health covers, and various services and professionals that may be available to help you work through any challenges. Hopefully, this was useful as you continue learning how to support yourself on your mental health journey.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h3","x":"References","isH2":false,"isH3":true,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Adams, R. (2003). Social work and empowerment.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Broderick, C. B., & Schrader, S. S. (1991). The history of professional marriage and family therapy. Handbook of family therapy, 2, 3-40.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Fagan, T. K., & Wise, P. S. (2000). School psychology: Past, present, and future. NASP Publications, 4340 East West Highway, Suite 402, Bethesda, MD 20814.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Galderisi, S., Heinz, A., Kastrup, M., Beezhold, J., & Sartorius, N. (2015). Toward a new definition of mental health. World psychiatry, 14(2), 231.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Kleinman, A. (2008). Rethinking psychiatry. Simon and Schuster.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Lambert, M. J., Bergin, A. E., & Garfield, S. L. (1994). The effectiveness of psychotherapy. Encyclopedia of psychotherapy, 1, 709-714.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"World Health Organization. (2004). Promoting mental health: Concepts, emerging evidence, practice: Summary report. World Health Organization.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false}],"read":"5 min read","num":"18"},{"title":"Why Western Culture Is Becoming More Entitled","dek":"Let’s learn about the psychology behind entitlement as a personality trait and how it can affect our lives and well-being.","cat":"culture","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"In psychology, entitlement refers to “a personality trait characterized by pervasive feelings of deservingness, specialness, and exaggerated expectations” (Grubbs & Exline, 2016). An entitled person believes they deserve special treatment, certain privileges, or additional resources (Lange et al., 2019). They typically believe that rules don’t apply to them. They think they deserve success, happiness, and respect without having to work for it and are often unwilling to acknowledge or appreciate the contributions of others.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"An entitlement personality is often linked to narcissistic tendencies and can show up in many ways. Here are some key characteristics associated with an entitlement personality (Campbell et al., 2004):","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Unrealistic expectations: People with an entitlement personality may have unrealistic expectations about what they deserve in things like relationships, work, or social interactions.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Lack of empathy: Entitled people often have difficulty understanding or caring about the needs and feelings of others. They may believe that their own needs are more important than the needs of others. They may also show less empathy and respect for romantic partners and engage in more game-playing and interpersonal aggression.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Difficulty accepting criticism: People with an entitlement personality may struggle to accept criticism or feedback, as they believe they are above reproach or free of faults.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Exploitative behavior: Some people with an entitlement personality may engage in exploitative behavior, taking advantage of others to meet their own needs or desires.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Grandiosity and superiority: Entitled people often see themselves as being better than other people. They may believe that they are smarter, more talented, or more important than others.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Impatience: An entitlement personality may be associated with impatience, as individuals may become frustrated when they don't get what they believe they deserve immediately.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"li","x":"Manipulative tactics: Some people with an entitlement personality may use manipulation to achieve their goals, disregarding the impact on others.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"li","x":"Difficulty with authority: Individuals with an entitlement personality may resist authority figures or rules that they feel limit their perceived entitlements.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":true,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Lack of accountability: People with an entitlement attitude may resist taking responsibility for their actions and failures. Instead, they might blame other people or external factors for their shortcomings.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"Entitlement Culture","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"Entitlement culture is when an entitlement mentality seems to be pervasive. It can show up not only in the majority of people, but also in song lyrics, on TV, and on social media. Some researchers believe that narcissism is spreading in our culture at an ever higher rate. And since entitlement is a component of narcissism, it would be included in this increase (Campbell & Twenge, 2013).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Although people with an entitled attitude have always existed, there is evidence that suggests Western culture may be becoming more entitled. This seems to be especially true of younger generations. A 2008 study found that people in their 20s were three times as likely to experience narcissistic personality disorder as people in their 60s (Campbell & Twenge, 2013).","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"There may be some changes in culture overall that are contributing to this shift. For example, there is a growing emphasis on individualism and self-reliance. This can lead to people feeling like they are entitled to success, even if they have not worked hard for it. Also, shifts in parenting styles, educational approaches, and cultural values can influence perceptions of entitlement.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Additionally, the rise of social media has created a culture where people are constantly comparing themselves to others. This can lead to feelings of inadequacy and resentment, which can fuel entitlement. The digital age has also brought about instant access to information, communication, and services. Some believe this contributes to a mindset of instant gratification, where we expect quick results and immediate rewards.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"h2","x":"In Sum","isH2":true,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":false},{"t":"p","x":"We all experience some feelings of entitlement, and sometimes these feelings are warranted. However, when entitlement becomes an overarching personality trait, it can have detrimental effects on our mental health, well-being, career, and relationships.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"It is important to remember that entitlement is a learned behavior. It can be caused by a variety of factors, such as parenting styles, peer pressure, and societal expectations. Once we understand the root causes of our entitlement, we can start to work on changing our behavior. And who knows? Curbing our sense of entitlement may even contribute to a more balanced and compassionate society where we have a sense of shared responsibility and respect for others.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"References","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Campbell, W. K., Bonacci, A. M., Shelton, J., Exline, J. J., & Bushman, B. J. (2004). Psychological entitlement: Interpersonal consequences and validation of a self-report measure. Journal of Personality Assessment, 83(1), 29–45.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Campbell, W. K., & Twenge, J. M. (2013, November 27). Narcissism unleashed. Association for Psychological Science. https://www.psychologicalscience.org/observer/narcissism-unleashed","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Grubbs, J. B., & Exline, J. J. (2016). Trait entitlement: A cognitive-personality source of vulnerability to psychological distress. Psychological Bulletin, 142(11), 1204.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true},{"t":"p","x":"Lange, J., Redford, L., & Crusius, J. (2019). A status-seeking account of psychological entitlement. Personality and Social Psychology Bulletin, 45(7), 1113–1128.","isH2":false,"isH3":false,"isLi":false,"isP":true}],"read":"4 min read","num":"19"}];